WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.342
LEFT JOIN is one of the most useful join patterns in analytics because it lets us preserve a complete

00:00:06.342 --> 00:00:09.992
population. Look at the small tables.

00:00:09.992 --> 00:00:13.197
Ana has two admissions, Cara has one, and Ben has none.

00:00:13.197 --> 00:00:18.622
With a LEFT JOIN, the left table is patients, so every patient must remain.

00:00:18.622 --> 00:00:22.772
Ana appears twice because she has two matching admissions.

00:00:22.772 --> 00:00:28.912
Cara appears once. Ben also remains, but the admission column is NULL because no admission matched.

00:00:28.912 --> 00:00:34.237
Now connect that visual to the PostgreSQL query.

00:00:34.237 --> 00:00:36.872
Patients appears before LEFT JOIN, so patients is the preserved side.

00:00:36.872 --> 00:00:43.042
Admissions is optional. The ON condition still controls matching by patient ID.

00:00:43.042 --> 00:00:45.522
This pattern is common in real projects.

00:00:45.522 --> 00:00:52.762
A university may want every enrolled student and an advising appointment when one exists.

00:00:52.762 --> 00:00:59.862
A business may want every customer and the customer's most recent order when available.

00:00:59.862 --> 00:01:04.842
A hospital may want every patient and a follow-up visit when available.

00:01:04.842 --> 00:01:10.902
If we used INNER JOIN in those situations, the people with no related activity would disappear.

00:01:10.902 --> 00:01:15.102
Run the query and find the patient with NULL admission values.

00:01:15.102 --> 00:01:18.247
That NULL is meaningful information.

00:01:18.247 --> 00:01:23.439
It tells us that the patient was preserved even though no related admission was found.
